  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nFog lingers in areas near Lake Superior this afternoon. Dense \nThis fog is expected to clear as a warm front makes its way \nnortheast. Temperatures for the rest of the day will push into \nthe upper 60s and 70s inland, though the shoreline will stay \nslightly cooler under the fog and cloud cover. A warm front will\nmove through today, shifting winds to the south to southwest and\nincreasing gusts to up to 30 mph tonight. Rain showers will \nalso follow the warm front in the late afternoon and overnight \nhours. Most will see rain accumulations of less then 0.1\".\n\nSaturday, the associated low pressure system tracking east will\nalso bring a cold front west to east across our area. This \nboundary will provide another round of light rain showers and \nperhaps an isolated rumble of thunder. Strong southerly flow\ncontinues ahead of the front will allow highs to climb into the\n60s and lower 70s once again, marking our last warm day for the\nforeseeable future. Rain chances will quickly taper off from \nwest to east Saturday night.\n\nA much cooler and more seasonable air mass settles in on Sunday\nbehind the departing cold front. Highs will only reach the 40s\nand 50s under mostly sunny skies as surface high pressure builds\nin over the area. RHs dip back down into the 25-35% range Sunday,\nbut with lower winds, no fire weather concerns are anticipated.\nThe cooler air mass will allow Sunday night temperatures to \ndrop below freezing for most inland locations.\n\nThe extended forecast for Monday through Thursday features a\nreturn to an active weather pattern. An upper level low is\nforecast to move east out of the central Rockies early next \nweek, bringing widespread precipitation chances to the region \nfrom Monday into Wednesday. With the cooler air mass firmly in \nplace, some snow could mix in on the northern fringes of the \nsystem, though this is currently expected to be minor \nconversational snow with small accumulations, if any. High \ntemperatures will largely remain in the 40s and 50s through \nThursday with overnight lows near or below freezing.",
